\subsection{Which Updates Can Be Skipped? (RQ1)}
Table~\ref{tab:policy} and Figure~\ref{fig:budget} compare the selection policies. Skipping matters: with no graph updates at all, event preservation drops to $F_{\mathrm{pres}}={{agn_C_none@0.0_test2024_fidF}}$, so the persistent state carries much of the information.

The skip-harm controller is the best deployable policy. With 25\% of the updates on the 2024 test set, it preserves the full model's events with $F_{\mathrm{pres}}={{agn_C_util@0.25_test2024_fidF}}$. AGN-Net's change objective reaches {{agn_C_change@0.25_test2024_fidF}}, energy {{agn_C_energy@0.25_test2024_fidF}}, periodic refresh {{agn_C_periodic@0.25_test2024_fidF}} and random selection {{agn_C_random@0.25_test2024_fidF}}. Our controller is close to the hindsight oracle ({{agn_C_oracle@0.25_test2024_fidF}}). The gain is positive in every seed, by {{ps_agn_test2024_0.25_util_random_fidF_min|2f}}--{{ps_agn_test2024_0.25_util_random_fidF_max|2f}} over random and {{ps_agn_test2024_0.25_util_change_fidF_min|2f}}--{{ps_agn_test2024_0.25_util_change_fidF_max|2f}} over the change objective. The patient-level bootstrap gives {{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_random_fidF_delta}} [{{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_random_fidF_lo}}, {{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_random_fidF_hi}}] and {{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_change_fidF_delta}} [{{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_change_fidF_lo}}, {{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_change_fidF_hi}}], respectively ($p{{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_change_fidF_p|p}}$). The same ordering holds at 12.5\% ({{agn_C_util@0.125_test2024_fidF}} vs.\ {{agn_C_change@0.125_test2024_fidF}} and {{agn_C_random@0.125_test2024_fidF}}) and at 50\% ({{agn_C_util@0.5_test2024_fidF}} vs.\ {{agn_C_change@0.5_test2024_fidF}} and {{agn_C_random@0.5_test2024_fidF}}). It also holds on the 2025 release ({{agn_C_util@0.25_test2025_fidF}} vs.\ {{agn_C_change@0.25_test2025_fidF}} and {{agn_C_random@0.25_test2025_fidF}} at 25\%) and for adventitious detections ($R^{\mathrm{adv}}_{\mathrm{pres}}={{agn_C_util@0.25_test2024_fid_advR}}$ vs.\ {{agn_C_change@0.25_test2024_fid_advR}} and {{agn_C_random@0.25_test2024_fid_advR}}). Note that these are the model's own adventitious detections, few of which match an annotated adventitious event under the shifted annotation style.

\paragraph*{Accuracy against the annotations}
Better preservation also gives better detection. At 25\%, event $F$ against the annotations is {{agn_C_util@0.25_test2024_F}} for our controller and {{agn_C_full_test2024_F}} with full updates. Random and periodic selection change it by {{bt_C_test2024_0.25_random_full_F_delta}} and {{bt_C_test2024_0.25_periodic_full_F_delta}} (bootstrap estimates on pooled counts). For our controller the difference to full updating is {{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_full_F_delta|4f}} [{{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_full_F_lo|4f}}, {{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_full_F_hi|4f}}]. Its lower bound is above the $-0.01$ margin, so selective inference with a quarter of the updates is non-inferior to full updating. The same holds on the 2025 release ({{bt_C_test2025_0.25_util_full_F_delta|4f}} [{{bt_C_test2025_0.25_util_full_F_lo|4f}}, {{bt_C_test2025_0.25_util_full_F_hi|4f}}]) and at 50\%. At 12.5\% the loss ({{bt_C_test2024_0.125_util_full_F_delta|4f}}) exceeds the margin, although it is half that of random selection.

\diagtable

\subsection{Why Does It Work? (RQ2)}
Table~\ref{tab:diag} measures how well each scorer ranks the 64 regions by their measured skip-harm utility. The skip-harm controller reaches a Spearman correlation of {{agn_diagC_util_rho}} and finds {{agn_diagC_util_topk|pct}}\% of the true top-25\% regions (random: {{agn_diagC_random_topk|pct}}\%). The change-trained head, with the same architecture and inputs, reaches only {{agn_diagC_change_rho}}. Acoustic change is even negatively correlated with utility ({{agn_diagC_acoustic_rho}}). This explains its ranking in Table~\ref{tab:policy}: it is the worst policy at every budget, well below random. Large spectral change is not a sign of an important update.

Utility is also concentrated in a few regions. The top 25\% of regions carry {{agn_diagC_conc_util|pct}}\% of the total utility but only {{agn_diagC_conc_change|pct}}\% of the representation change. Because the KL-based utility grows roughly quadratically, we also compare on matched scales. The top quarter still carries {{agn_diagC_conc_util_sqrt|pct}}\% of the square-root utility against {{agn_diagC_conc_change|pct}}\% of the change. This concentration is what makes small budgets work: the updates that matter are few, even though the representation changes almost everywhere. Figure~\ref{fig:example} shows an example. Utility concentrates in the lower-frequency regions, which our controller updates persistently, while the change-trained controller spreads its updates widely.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.72\textwidth]{figures/example.pdf}
\caption{Example 2024 test recording at a 25\% budget (seed 0). (a) Log-mel spectrogram with the annotated events (bars; W: wheeze, N: normal). (b) Each region's share of the measured skip-harm utility per step. (c) Nodes updated (dark) by our controller. (d) Nodes updated by the change-trained controller.}
\label{fig:example}
\end{figure}

\ifgg
\subsection{Transfer to Another Graph Cell (RQ3)}
\label{sec:gg}
We repeated the selection pipeline, with the same hyperparameters, on the plain graph-GRU cell, the strongest full-update backbone (Section~\ref{sec:backbones}). Table~\ref{tab:policygg} shows the same picture. At 25\%, the skip-harm controller preserves $F_{\mathrm{pres}}={{ggrusel_C_util@0.25_test2024_fidF}}$, against {{ggrusel_C_change@0.25_test2024_fidF}} for the change objective, {{ggrusel_C_periodic@0.25_test2024_fidF}} for periodic and {{ggrusel_C_random@0.25_test2024_fidF}} for random. It wins in every seed, by {{ps_ggrusel_test2024_0.25_util_change_fidF_min|2f}}--{{ps_ggrusel_test2024_0.25_util_change_fidF_max|2f}} over the change objective. This cell relies even more on its state (preservation {{ggrusel_C_none@0.0_test2024_fidF}} without updates), and its utility is even more concentrated ({{ggrusel_diagC_conc_util|pct}}\% in the top quarter). Against the annotations, event $F$ at 25\% is {{ggrusel_C_util@0.25_test2024_F}} versus {{ggrusel_C_full_test2024_F}} with full updates ({{bt_ggC_test2024_0.25_util_full_F_delta|4f}} [{{bt_ggC_test2024_0.25_util_full_F_lo|4f}}, {{bt_ggC_test2024_0.25_util_full_F_hi|4f}}]). Non-inferiority is therefore narrowly missed at 25\% on the 2024 set, but it holds on the 2025 release and at 50\%. The advantage of the skip-harm target is thus not specific to the AGN-Net cell.

\policytable{tables/policy_gg_C.tex}{Selection policies for the plain graph-GRU cell (same setup as Table~\ref{tab:policy}).}{tab:policygg}
\fi

\subsection{Ablation Study (RQ4)}
\paragraph*{Training target} All three utility targets clearly beat the change target (Table~\ref{tab:policy}). The hold-only target is about as good as the full two-endpoint target: slightly better at 12.5\% ({{agn_C_util_hold@0.125_test2024_fidF}} vs.\ {{agn_C_util@0.125_test2024_fidF}}) and slightly worse at 25\% ({{agn_C_util_hold@0.25_test2024_fidF}} vs.\ {{agn_C_util@0.25_test2024_fidF}}). Removing the label term costs {{bt_C_test2024_0.25_util_util_kl_fidF_delta}} $F_{\mathrm{pres}}$ at 25\% (bootstrap estimate). What matters most is that the target measures the effect on the output.

\paragraph*{Refresh rule} Forcing refreshes of stale states uses part of the budget. Without it, $F_{\mathrm{pres}}$ is {{agn_C_util_norefresh@0.125_test2024_fidF}} instead of {{agn_C_util@0.125_test2024_fidF}} at 12.5\% and {{agn_C_util_norefresh@0.25_test2024_fidF}} instead of {{agn_C_util@0.25_test2024_fidF}} at 25\%. On these short recordings (at most 15\,s) the rule costs a little at the smallest budget. Its benefit, protection against arbitrarily old states, would only appear on longer streams.

\paragraph*{Selective fine-tuning} Before fine-tuning (stage-A weights, Table~\ref{tab:policyA}), preservation is lower for every policy ({{agn_A_util@0.25_test2024_fidF}} for ours and {{agn_A_random@0.25_test2024_fidF}} for random at 25\%). The utility heads are still best on average, but the ordering varies across seeds. Fine-tuning under selective rollouts with distillation raises our controller to {{agn_C_util@0.25_test2024_fidF}}, while random rises only to {{agn_C_random@0.25_test2024_fidF}}. Part of this gain may come from the fine-tuning mixture, which samples the utility-based policies more often.

\paragraph*{Fresh-feature path} Without the fresh features in the readout (Table~\ref{tab:policynf}), the model cannot react to the current chunk unless states are updated. Selection therefore matters even more: at 25\%, our controller preserves {{agnnf_C_util@0.25_test2024_fidF}}, against {{agnnf_C_random@0.25_test2024_fidF}} for random. The fresh path makes selective inference more forgiving, but our advantage does not depend on it.

\policytable{tables/policy_A.tex}{Selection policies on the stage-A weights (before selective fine-tuning). Columns, bold and $^\dagger$ as in Table~\ref{tab:policy}.}{tab:policyA}
\policytable{tables/policy_nf_C.tex}{Selection policies for the model without the fresh-feature path in the readout. Columns, bold and $^\dagger$ as in Table~\ref{tab:policy}.}{tab:policynf}

