\subsection{Efficiency (RQ5)}
\label{sec:h3}
Table~\ref{tab:runtime} and Figure~\ref{fig:runtime} report streaming latency on one CPU core. All configurations run far faster than real time: a full update takes {{rt_numpy_full@1.0_mean_ms|2f}}\,ms per 40\,ms chunk in the NumPy runtime, of which {{rt_numpy_full@1.0_graph|2f}}\,ms is the graph update. With our controller at 25\%, the graph update drops to {{rt_numpy_util@0.25_graph|2f}}\,ms, but the controller adds {{rt_numpy_util@0.25_selection|2f}}\,ms. The net speedup is {{rt_numpy_util@0.125_speedup|2f}}$\times$ at 12.5\%, {{rt_numpy_util@0.25_speedup|2f}}$\times$ at 25\% and {{rt_numpy_util@0.5_speedup|2f}}$\times$ at 50\%. Random and periodic selection are faster ({{rt_numpy_random@0.25_speedup|2f}}$\times$ and {{rt_numpy_periodic@0.25_speedup|2f}}$\times$ at 25\%) because their scores are almost free, but they preserve far fewer events. Removing the graph entirely gives {{rt_numpy_none@0.0_speedup|2f}}$\times$, an upper bound on the possible gain. In the PyTorch runtime, operator-launch overhead dominates the small graph, and selective inference is slower than full updating ({{rt_torch_util@0.25_speedup|2f}}$\times$). The efficiency gain is therefore real but modest, and it depends on a lean runtime and on keeping the controller cheap.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/runtime_numpy.pdf}
\caption{Per-chunk latency by stage in the NumPy runtime (one CPU core, mean over seeds).}
\label{fig:runtime}
\end{figure}

\archtable

\subsection{Comparison of Full-Update Backbones}
\label{sec:backbones}
Table~\ref{tab:arch} compares the backbones when every node is updated at every step (stage-A weights). Graph-structured state helps: the plain graph GRU is the best model, with event $F={{ggru_test2024_F}}$ and seg-adv-$F={{ggru_test2024_seg_advF}}$ on the 2024 set, ahead of the GRU, the TCN and memoryless pooling. Our AGN-Net cell, however, is not better than the plain graph GRU. Its mean event $F$ is {{agn_test2024_F}}, with a large variance across seeds: one seed is the best model in the study, while the other two are among the weakest. Absolute scores are low for all models, mainly because of the annotation shift between training and test data. Offline detectors designed for this benchmark, such as EZhouNet~\cite{chu2026ezhounet}, report higher event-level scores. Our claims concern selective computation relative to a given model, not state-of-the-art detection accuracy.
